          "justification": "Use Chopsticks evaluates a system's ability to perform quasistatic indirect manipulation through a pair of tools while handling small objects that are significantly smaller than the hand. The task involves manipulating two rigid chopsticks and a set of rigid cubes, requiring coordinated control of multiple objects simultaneously. Relative to direct grasping tasks, the use of chopsticks introduces additional degrees of freedom associated with controlling the relative motion and contact forces between the chopsticks while maintaining stable object capture. Successful completion requires precise alignment of the chopsticks, careful force regulation to prevent dropping or crushing the object, and accurate placement of the object into a target container. It also involves repeated constraint change, progressing through cycles of tool positioning, object acquisition, object transport, and object release. More broadly, chopstick use serves as a representative benchmark for precision tool use and indirect manipulation, assessing a manipulator's ability to perform fine object handling through intermediate tools rather than direct hand-object contact.",
